\section*{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Single dataset and annotation quality.}
All CP-dependent claims rely on one dataset (HarmThoughts~\citep{harmthoughts}), the only publicly available dataset providing step-level CP annotations for reasoning model traces, with annotations lacking formal inter-annotator agreement; an LLM-based proxy on AdvBench achieved $\kappa{=}0.606$ (\cref{app:iaa}). The geometric precondition (shallow-layer compression) is confirmed on AdvBench (\cref{app:cosine_volatility,app:advbench_cp}), but generalization to other jailbreak datasets, attack taxonomies, or multilingual settings is untested.

\paragraph{Statistical power at 32B.}
With $n{=}19$--$20$ traces, the minimum detectable effect at 80\% power is 8.6--9.5pp (\cref{app:power_analysis}); all 32B conclusions marked $\dagger$ should be interpreted with this constraint. False positive rates remain 11--50\% (model-dependent), and R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ (25\% coverage). These probes characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than constitute production detectors.

\paragraph{Architecture coverage and causal status.}
Three of four models share the Qwen architecture; only R1-8B (Llama) provides cross-architecture evidence. Random-label controls confirm the crossing-rate geometric bias is Qwen-specific (\cref{app:random_cp}). Results are correlational; causal intervention (e.g., DAS, activation patching) is needed to confirm mechanistic involvement. Whether metric-induced layer selection bias generalizes to non-reasoning LLMs or encoder-only architectures remains open.

\section*{Ethics Statement}
This work analyzes safety-relevant reasoning traces to characterize probe behavior, not to circumvent safety mechanisms. All experiments use publicly available models and datasets (HarmThoughts, AdvBench). No human subjects were involved. Code and scripts for all experiments and analyses will be released upon acceptance.

\subsection{Multi-Metric Layer Ensemble}
\label{app:ensemble}

For each model, we train logistic regression probes at the best layer selected by each of three threshold-based metrics (precision, BA, inverse FPR) and average their predicted probabilities to produce an ensemble score. This eliminates the need to pre-select a single metric for layer selection.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\caption{Multi-metric layer ensemble vs.\ best single metric and text baseline. Ensemble trains probes at 3 threshold-based metric-selected layers and averages probabilities. $\Delta$Ens--Best: ensemble minus best single metric (negative = ensemble slightly lower). All Ens--Text differences are significant ($p \leq 0.002$, permutation test). No Ens--Best difference is significant ($p > 0.27$).}
\label{tab:ensemble}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Ens Prec} & \textbf{Best Single} & $\Delta$ & \textbf{Ens$-$Text} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & 0.565 & 0.604 (BA@L12) & $-$3.9pp & +19.3pp \\
OT-7B & 0.506 & 0.473 (FPR@L18) & +3.3pp & +27.0pp \\
QwQ-32B & 0.578 & 0.578 (Prec@L60) & $-$0.0pp & +20.3pp \\
R1-32B & 0.581 & 0.583 (Prec@L63) & $-$0.2pp & +10.5pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

This ensemble is a preliminary exploration of metric-agnostic layer selection, not a validated mitigation strategy; rigorous cross-validated evaluation across diverse datasets is future work. The ensemble provides a metric-agnostic selection strategy among principled (threshold-based) metrics. It never significantly underperforms the best individual metric (all $p > 0.27$), and all four models show significant HS advantage over text ($p \leq 0.002$). The ensemble advantage over text ranges from +10.5pp (R1-32B) to +27.0pp (OT-7B). This proof of concept validates that practitioners need not commit to a single metric for layer selection; averaging across metric-selected layers achieves comparable performance. Future work should test whether including biased metrics (e.g., crossing-rate composite) in the ensemble degrades performance, and whether learned metric weighting could improve upon uniform averaging.

