\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

\paragraph{Exploratory 32B analysis.} Both 32B results are exploratory ($n{=}19$--$20$ precludes nested CV). LOO-CV and multi-seed analyses (\cref{app:layer_sweep,app:multi_seed}) provide partial robustness checks but do not substitute for independent validation. R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}); training method is one plausible differentiator. Replication with $n{\geq}50$ traces is needed.\looseness=-1

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Cov.} & \textbf{Lead (steps)} & \textbf{HB} & \textbf{Pos.\ margin} \\
\midrule
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & 25\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & \checkmark{} ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\
QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ & 74\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & \checkmark{} (all $W$) & $-$3.6pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{32B cross-model divergence ($\dagger$\,sample-limited, $n \leq 20$; MDE at 80\% power: 8.6--9.5pp, \cref{app:power_analysis}; all conclusions exploratory). R1-32B provides limited early identification with narrow coverage; QwQ-32B provides robust significance with poor lead time. HB: \checkmark{} = passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$). Pos.\ margin: HS BA minus position baseline.}
\label{tab:32b_divergence}
\end{table}

\end{table}

R1-32B suggests commitment 11--13 steps early (+14.2pp over position baseline) but covers only 25\% of traces ($W{=}1$); the precision effect (+15.4pp, CI width 13.9pp) falls below MDE at 80\% power. QwQ-32B achieves significance at all window sizes with 74\% coverage but negative HS lead time; the precision effect (+19.2pp, CI width 24.2pp) exceeds MDE (8.6--9.5pp; \cref{app:power_analysis}) but the position baseline achieves higher BA (0.832 vs.\ 0.796), and the HS precision margin over position (+4.5pp, $p{=}0.133$; \cref{app:position_residual}) is not significant at $n{=}19$. The divergence suggests per-model validation is needed.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities (\cref{fig:window_ablation}): at 7--8B, the modality advantage (+20.1 to +21.9pp) exceeds temporal improvement (+13pp). GRU models achieve comparable accuracy but higher FPR (\cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds,app:temporal_details}); an adaptive two-stage strategy offers marginal gains (\cref{app:two_stage}).\looseness=-1

